`]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Why language models read subwords",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Every text a computer stores is already a sequence of bytes. So why does a language model not read those bytes directly? The answer decides how long the model's sequences are, how large its embedding tables become, and what structure in a text the model can see. Training, attention, optimization and scaling all operate on the token sequence that comes out."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Week 1 traced the path from text to model input. A tokenizer cuts the text into pieces, called tokens, and replaces each piece with an integer ID. Each ID selects one row of the embedding table, and that row is the vector the model works with. When the model generates text, the tokenizer maps the generated IDs back to text. This reverse step is called decoding."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The table compares three choices of unit on one sentence, the English sentence of the comparison widget in the Vocabulary tab. The trace below cuts it in all three ways."}),e.jsx(t.figure,{"data-rehype-pretty-code-figure":"",children:e.jsx(t.pre,{tabIndex:"0","data-language":"plaintext","data-theme":"github-dark-default",children:e.jsxs(t.code,{"data-language":"plaintext","data-theme":"github-dark-default",style:{display:"grid"},children:[e.jsx(t.span,{"data-line":"",children:e.jsx(t.span,{children:'"The transformer architecture revolutionized natural language processing."'})}),`
