`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"What a larger vocabulary buys and costs",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A larger vocabulary can hold longer pieces, so the same corpus may need fewer tokens. That means shorter sequences and more text per context window. But every new row has to be trained, and a fixed corpus now spreads its training signal across more piece types, so rare pieces see few examples. Quality on rare pieces and across languages therefore need not improve steadily as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"V"})," grows."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Read the table one row at a time. Each row says what changes, how, and how to measure it. The tables grow by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"V · d_model"})," each, which costs parameters and memory. Sequence length may fall, which you measure as token count and use of the effective context, the amount of text that a context window of fixed length holds. Piece frequency spreads out, which you measure as frequency and fragmentation. No vocabulary size is best everywhere, so the decision rests on samples of the target languages and domains."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Compare candidates on the target data mixture itself, by sequence length, piece frequency, and behaviour per language and domain ",e.jsx(n,{id:"fineweb2-2025",children:"Penedo et al., 2025"}),". Then measure throughput, effective context and downstream quality."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["A tokenizer change in a deployed model shows the token-count side of this trade-off from the user's side. When Anthropic released Claude Opus 4.7 in April 2026, it gave the model an updated tokenizer that, in Anthropic's words, improves how the model processes text. The stated price is that the same input can map to more tokens, roughly 1.0 to 1.35 times as many depending on the content type ",e.jsx(n,{id:"anthropic2026opus47",children:"Anthropic, 2026"}),". Anthropic does not say how the vocabulary changed, and it kept the per-token prices of the previous model. API use is billed and limited in tokens, so the same prompt can cost more and fill more of the context window than under the previous tokenizer."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Compare tokenizers on one text, not languages on different texts",accent:2,number:"12",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
