`]}),e.jsx(p,{}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"The same text can cost very different token counts",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The widget runs three tokenizers on three sentences, one English, one Japanese and one Bengali. The tokenizers are ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"cl100k_base"}),", the OpenAI tokenizer of GPT-4 and GPT-3.5 Turbo ",e.jsx(n,{id:"tiktoken-0.12.0",children:"OpenAI, 2025"}),", Llama 2's tokenizer ",e.jsx(n,{id:"llama2-tokenizer-artifact",children:"Meta, 2023"}),", and Monad. The length of a token sequence depends strongly on the tokenizer and on the kind of text. The English sentence takes 9 or 10 tokens under all three. The Japanese sentence takes 26 tokens under ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"cl100k_base"}),", 27 under Llama 2 and 66 under Monad. The Bengali sentence takes 93, 104 and 217. On the Japanese and Bengali sentences, Monad needs more than twice as many tokens as the other two, up to about 2.5 times as many (66 against 26)."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Token counts are costs. Training and inference cost grow with the number of tokens processed, so text that a tokenizer cuts into many short pieces is more expensive to train on and to serve. A context window of fixed length holds less of it, and the model has to connect information across more positions, which makes long contexts harder. Petrov et al. measured this across languages and found that the same text, translated into different languages, can differ up to 15-fold in token count ",e.jsx(n,{id:"petrov2023unfairness",children:"Petrov et al., 2023"}),". So if your model must handle a particular domain or language well, for example a low-resource language with little training text, you need a tokenizer whose merges cover it. Otherwise training and inference on that text cost much more than on English."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Quality probably suffers too, though the widget does not measure it. An embedding row learns only from the positions that select it. When a script falls apart into single bytes, the same few byte tokens appear inside many unrelated characters and words. Each of their rows then has to serve many meanings at once, and the model has to assemble every character from several positions before it can work with the word."}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Read the counts within one sentence. The sentences say different things, so the widget compares different tokenizers on the same sentence. A count for the Japanese sentence and a count for the English one measure different content. They cannot show that a tokenizer handles one language more efficiently than another. That claim needs the same content in both languages, called parallel text, and a large enough sample, as in Petrov et al.'s study."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Within one sentence the rows also show how Monad falls back to bytes. For the Japanese and Bengali sentences its token count equals the byte count, 66 and 217, so every token is a single byte. The widget shows each of these bytes as its GPT-2 display symbol, so ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"å"})," or ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"¤"})," in Monad's row is one byte, not a Latin letter. No Monad vocabulary entry, whole or partial, contains the multi-byte sequences of these two scripts, so no merge applies. The English sentence, by contrast, goes from 72 bytes to 10 tokens. The other rows show partial characters in their own ways, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"cl100k_base"})," as hexadecimal pieces and Llama 2 as byte-fallback tokens such as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"<0xE5>"}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
