`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"What the arithmetic evidence shows",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Singh and Strouse tested arithmetic in GPT models under different number formats. In the models they studied, how token boundaries aligned with the digits, and whether digits were grouped from the left or from the right, materially changed arithmetic accuracy ",e.jsx(n,{id:"singh2024",children:"Singh & Strouse, 2024"}),". Token boundaries are therefore part of the learning problem. The evidence names no digit policy that is best for every model. It also leaves open whether one-digit tokens generalize better in general and whether multi-digit tokens push a model toward memorizing. Llama 3's one-to-three-digit chunks are one policy among several."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"If your system handles numbers, test the tokenizer on them. Build a regression set from the formats and lengths the system will serve, with signed values, decimals, thousands separators, and every length it will see. Check how each candidate tokenizer splits them, and measure the task itself on them."})]})]})]}),`
