`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Word segmentation depends on the language"}),`
`]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Why no single denominator wins",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Fertility divides by words. Other diagnostics divide by bytes, by Unicode code points (the numbered characters of the Unicode standard), or by graphemes (what a reader perceives as one character). In ASCII text, bytes and code points are equal, so an English example cannot show that the choice matters."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The Japanese sentence of the comparison widget can. It has 22 code points and 66 UTF-8 bytes, because each of its characters takes three bytes. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"cl100k_base"})," encodes it in 26 tokens: 26/66 = 0.394 tokens per byte, but 26/22 = 1.182 tokens per code point. Per byte, the tokenizer looks compact. Per code point, it needs more than one token. Both numbers are correct, and they answer different questions. Word segmentation depends on the language as well. Japanese does not separate words with spaces, so fertility needs a segmenter built for it."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Choose the denominator that fits the decision. Per byte fits a budget counted in bytes, such as storage or the size of the raw input. Per code point or grapheme fits the length of the text as a reader sees it. A report that gives only one denominator hides what the others would show."})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Reuse a tokenizer unless measurements justify training one",accent:1,number:"19",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
