`]}),e.jsxs(t.table,{children:[e.jsx(t.thead,{children:e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Situation"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Starting point"}),e.jsx(t.th,{children:"Entries"})]})}),e.jsxs(t.tbody,{children:[e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Very small model, English only"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Monad"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"8,192"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Medium-sized model"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Llama 2"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"32,000"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Medium-sized model"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.code,{children:"cl100k_base"})}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"~100k"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Many languages, larger tables"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:e.jsx(t.code,{children:"o200k_base"})}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"~200k"})]}),e.jsxs(t.tr,{children:[e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Many languages, larger tables"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"Gemma 4"}),e.jsx(t.td,{children:"~262k"})]})]})]}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"Four questions before changing a tokenizer",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A tokenizer choice affects the data, the model, the system and the results, so four questions cover it. For the data, does the tokenizer represent the target languages, scripts, domains and number formats? For the model, what do its vocabulary tables and sequence lengths cost at the intended width and context? For the system, what throughput and effective context does it give on the actual hardware? For the outcome, what changes in downstream loss and task quality?"}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In practice you load an existing tokenizer through a library. Hugging Face's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"transformers"})," library loads the tokenizer that belongs to a model repository with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained"})," ",e.jsx(n,{id:"hf-autotokenizer",children:"Hugging Face, 2026"}),", and the course uses it as a thin wrapper around Monad. OpenAI's tiktoken provides OpenAI's encodings, such as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"cl100k_base"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"o200k_base"}),", the tokenizer of GPT-4o ",e.jsx(n,{id:"tiktoken-0.12.0",children:"OpenAI, 2025"}),". Gigatoken is a newer library that reimplements common byte-pair tokenizers for very fast encoding of large corpora. Through a compatibility mode it can stand in for Hugging Face's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"tokenizers"})," library and for tiktoken. Its author reports encoding speeds of gigabytes per second on a many-core server, about a thousand times faster than ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"tokenizers"})," ",e.jsx(n,{id:"gigatoken2026",children:"Rød, 2026"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Which tokenizer to start from depends mostly on the model's size and its languages. The table gives our suggestions, each to be checked with the four questions. A very small model trained on English only can use a small vocabulary such as Monad's 8,192 entries, which keeps its tables cheap. A medium-sized model can use Llama 2's 32,000-entry tokenizer or ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"cl100k_base"})," with about 100,000 entries. A model that must cover many languages and scripts needs room for merges in all of them, so it uses a larger vocabulary such as ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"o200k_base"}),", with about 200,000 entries, or Gemma 4's, with about 262,000 ",e.jsx(n,{id:"gemma4-2026",children:"Gemma Team, 2026"}),", and pays for it with larger embedding tables."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Start from a suitable existing tokenizer. Training a new one, or adapting an existing one, costs more than the training run. The embedding and output tables change, models and data prepared for the old tokenizer no longer match, and everything has to be validated again. Train or adapt only when measured gains justify those costs ",e.jsx(n,{id:"dagan2024",children:"Dagan et al., 2024"}),". A shorter corpus is a reason to measure further. If, for illustration, a domain tokenizer shortens your target corpus by 12%, also measure throughput, effective context and downstream quality, and keep the tokenizer you have if those do not improve."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Raw bytes cost length; subwords buy it back",question:"So why not feed the model raw bytes?",accent:2,number:"20",items:"5",promptitems:"0",children:[e.jsx(h,{children:e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Course text: 4.17 bytes per token"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Merges trade vocabulary rows for length"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Full coverage needs all 256 bytes"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Monad keeps 207; course text unaffected"}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Course tokenizer: Monad, 8,192 entries ",e.jsx(n,{id:"monad-2025",children:"PleIAs, 2025"})]}),`
`]})}),e.jsxs(a,{title:"The answer, and the course's choice",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"So why not feed the model raw bytes? Raw bytes cover every input, but they cost one position per byte. On the course's English training text, Monad averages 4.17 bytes per token. With byte input, the same text would need about four times as many positions, with the attention work that comes with them, and a context window of fixed length would hold about a quarter as much text. Byte-level BPE keeps the coverage of bytes, as long as all 256 byte symbols stay in the vocabulary, and spends vocabulary rows to buy shorter sequences. How many rows, which merges, and whether every byte symbol is kept are choices you measure on the target data."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Monad shows both sides on the comparison widget's sentences. Its 7,981 learned merges take the English sentence from 72 bytes to 10 tokens. The Japanese and Bengali sentences stay at one token per byte, because no Monad entry, whole or partial, covers their byte sequences. Monad also keeps only 207 of the 256 byte symbols, yet the course's English training text produced no ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"[UNK]"})," in about 20 million tokens."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The course reuses Monad with its 8,192 logical vocabulary entries ",e.jsx(n,{id:"monad-2025",children:"PleIAs, 2025"}),". Reusing a tokenizer lets the course spend its time on the model, training and optimization, while you can still measure what segmentation does. Another language, domain, context budget or deployment target may call for a different tokenizer."]})]})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Six results on tokenization",accent:3,number:"21",items:"6",children:[e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Subwords trade vocabulary size for length"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"BPE learns ranks; encoding replays them"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Check byte coverage, chunks and normalization"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Vocabulary rows cost parameters, shorten sequences"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Number boundaries change the arithmetic to learn"}),`
`,e.jsx(t.li,{children:"Measure on target data before choosing"}),`
`]}),e.jsx(a,{title:"The chapter in six results",children:e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Subwords trade vocabulary size for sequence length. Whole words leave unseen words unknown, and raw bytes make sequences long. BPE training learns a ranked list of merges, and encoding replays that list in rank order on new text. A byte-level base leaves no input unknown only if all 256 byte symbols are in the vocabulary. Pre-tokenization decides where merges can happen, and normalization can still change a text on its round trip. Every vocabulary row costs ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"d_model"})," parameters per table and can buy shorter sequences, at the price of a thinner training signal per row. Number boundaries change the structure a model has to learn for arithmetic, so test the formats a system serves. Fertility, PCW and per-byte or per-code-point measures describe a tokenizer on a named sample. Target tasks and runtime measurements decide whether to keep a tokenizer or to train a new one."]})})]}),e.jsxs(s,{title:"Reflection questions",accent:4,number:"22",items:"4",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"No submission. Bring short answers to the Week 3 tutorial."}),e.jsxs(t.ol,{children:[`
